# Introduction

Before we look at the clustering models, it's useful to explore the candidate variables. Unlike supervised learning, clustering has no outcome variable, so exploratory analysis focuses on understanding the structure of the feature space. The objectives are to identify missing values, examine variable distributions, detect highly correlated features, identify potential transformations, and ensure that no single concept is disproportionately represented in the clustering algorithm. *TODO: add ref??*

Exploratory analysis is a standard part of clustering workflows because clustering algorithms are sensitive to the representation of the feature space. Examining variable distributions, identifying outliers, assessing correlations between variables, and applying appropriate transformations and standardisation can improve the interpretability and stability of cluster solutions (Tan, Steinbach & Kumar, 2019; Kaufman & Rousseeuw, 2005).

In predictive modelling you often ask: *Does this variable improve prediction?*  
In clustering we ask: *Does this variable help distinguish meaningful neighbourhood types?*   


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Packages
library(tidyverse)
library(here)
library(targets)
library(gt)
library(gtExtras)
library(sf)
library(leaflet)
# library(osdatahub)
library(tidymodels)
# library(tidyclust)
library(skimr)
library(ggcorrplot)
library(factoextra)

# Sources all R/*.R same as _targets.R
tar_source()       

# Objects needed from our {targets} pipeline
tar_load(df_lsoa_all)
tar_load(sf_lsoa)
tar_load(sf_ngd_highway)
tar_load(sf_city)

# ggplot theme
set_theme(theme_minimal())
