The spatial pattern reflects a combination of the arterial road network and geographically large LSOAs in the Peak District (LSOAs cover approximately equal population sizes rather than equal geographic sizes).

# Distribution plots

Distribution plots for key variables. There are different approaches for visually exploring data^[Feature Engineering and Selection - 4.2 Visualizations for Numeric Data ([feat.engineering/04-Exploratory_Visualizations.html#visualizations-for-numeric-data-exploring-train-ridership-data](https://feat.engineering/04-Exploratory_Visualizations.html#visualizations-for-numeric-data-exploring-train-ridership-data))]. 

## Cycling  

- `cycle_infra_length_m`  
- `cycle_infra_density_m_per_km2` 

::: {.callout-note}
## Summary  
The cycling infrastructure variables will not be included in our model, unless/until we can improve it to more accurately reflect the intended dimension.   
:::

Initially we tried violin and beeswarm plots but these weren't suitable for the large proportion of zero values.    


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
n_lsoa <- df_lsoa |> 
  count() |> 
  pull()

n_zero_cycle_infra_length_m <- df_lsoa |> 
  filter(cycle_infra_length_m == 0) |> 
  count() |> 
  pull()

ggplot(df_lsoa, aes(x = cycle_infra_length_m)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 20, colour = "white") +
  scale_x_continuous(
    trans   = "log1p",
    breaks  = c(0, 10, 100, 500, 1500, 3500),
    labels  = scales::comma
  ) +
  labs(
    x = "Cycle infrastructure length (m)",
    y = "LSOAs",
    title = str_c(
      round((n_zero_cycle_infra_length_m/n_lsoa)*100),
      "% of LSOAs (",
      n_zero_cycle_infra_length_m, "/", n_lsoa, 
      ") have zero recorded cycling infrastructure"
    ),
    subtitle = str_c(
      "The x-axis is shown on a natural logarithmic scale so that the\n",
      n_lsoa - n_zero_cycle_infra_length_m,
      " non-zero LSOAs in the sparse tail can be viewed more clearly"
    )
  )
```

